<p>The Netherlands took about 28% of India's grape tonnage but about 39% of the value, paying about 41% more per kilo than the all-market average. A unit value averages every variety, size and contract, so it is not a price, but the pattern is consistent: the UK, the Netherlands and Germany sit together at the top, while Bangladesh and Nepal, the two short-haul neighbours, take large volumes at a fraction of the value. EU-bound fruit carries costs the neighbouring trade does not: residue testing to EU MRLs, export-grade sorting by berry size, forced-air pre-cooling, SO2 pads and an 18-24 day reefer voyage. Dutch buyers themselves split between importers re-distributing across the EU and packers running supermarket punnet programmes; the second group pays for the tighter specification.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>Which grape varieties and berry sizes should you specify?</h2>
<p>Most of India's export grapes are grown around Nashik and Sangli in Maharashtra. Thompson Seedless is the export mainstay, with Flame Seedless and (seasonally) Crimson Seedless covering red-grape demand, and Sonaka — a Thompson selection — also widely available. Specify berry size (16-18mm or 18mm+) and bunch weight expectations (typically 300-500g); mixed sizing within a carton is a common quality complaint on under-specified orders. TSS (sweetness, measured in degrees Brix) typically runs 16-18° at export maturity — tell us if your market expects a specific sweetness profile.</p>

<h2>What actually determines arrival quality on this route</h2>
<p>Given the 18-24 day transit to Rotterdam — the longest we quote at real volume for a perishable — three things matter more here than almost anywhere else:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pre-cooling speed.</strong> Field heat removed slowly costs shelf life that can't be recovered later, however well the container is subsequently managed.</li>
  <li><strong>SO2 pad calibration.</strong> Sulphur dioxide generator pads inside the carton suppress botrytis (grey mould) over the long sea transit. Too little and mould develops; too much and bleaching occurs. Packing needs to match the actual transit length.</li>
  <li><strong>Consistent 0-2°C storage.</strong> No margin for temperature excursions on a route this long.</li>
</ul>

<!-- insight:seasonality -->
<h2>How much shelf life is left when grapes reach Rotterdam?</h2>
<p>Less than the headline figure suggests. Well-handled grapes hold 45-60 days at 0-2°C with SO2 pads, and the voyage to Rotterdam takes 18-24 of those days. That leaves roughly 21-42 days for discharge, CHED-PP clearance, distribution and the retail shelf, before allowing for the days between pre-cooling and sailing. Fruit that was pre-cooled late starts that count already short.</p>
<p>The season is fixed at the other end. India ships January to April, peaking in February and March. Scenario: a Dutch importer confirms an order on 1 April. With 7-12 days ex-packhouse, the container sails around 8-13 April and reaches Rotterdam around 26 April to 7 May, when late-season fruit is softer and Southern Hemisphere supply is still in the market. Programmes that need consistent quality book their volume for February and March sailings and treat April as top-up.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>EU maximum residue limits are set by Regulation (EC) 396/2005, and any pesticide without a specific EU MRL for grapes defaults to 0.01 mg/kg, which in practice means "not detectable". They are strict and actively enforced at the border, and pesticide residue is the single most common cause of Indian fresh produce rejection entering the EU. We strongly recommend agreeing pre-shipment residue testing before the goods ship rather than treating it as something to sort out if a problem arises. {{ site.complianceChecklistCta }}</p>
<p>Phytosanitary certificates for EU-bound plant products are registered through the EU's TRACES NT platform, using a CHED-PP (Common Health Entry Document for Plants and Plant Products) entry. This is typically managed on the importer's side, using the certificate documentation we provide — worth confirming the process with your importer before your first shipment if this is new territory for either side. In the Netherlands, official checks on plant products arriving at Rotterdam are carried out by the NVWA.</p>
<p>A separate EU instrument can raise the inspection rate. Implementing Regulation (EU) 2019/1793 lists food of non-animal origin from named countries that faces a temporarily increased frequency of identity and physical checks (Annex I), or special import conditions such as an official certificate with sampling results (Annex II), mostly for pesticide residues. The lists are amended regularly on the basis of official control results, so entries are added and removed over time. Check the current consolidated annex before each season to see whether table grapes from India are listed and at what check frequency.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>Grape pricing moves more within a single season than almost any other product we handle, and we don't quote a flat per-carton number without knowing your specification and timing. Five things matter most: where you are in the January-April season (early and late-season fruit price differently than peak February-March volume, driven by both quality and simple supply), berry size and bunch-weight grade (18mm+ commands a premium over 16-18mm), variety (Thompson Seedless, Flame Seedless and Crimson Seedless don't price identically — red-grape varieties often carry a different demand curve than green), packaging format (retail punnets with custom artwork cost more per tonne than bulk cartons), and prevailing INR/EUR exchange rates plus reefer freight rates to Rotterdam, which are a meaningfully larger share of landed cost for grapes than for most non-perishable exports because of the cold-chain requirement.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific variety, berry-size grade, packaging format and delivery week — never as a flat seasonal-average rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: variety preference, berry size grade, packaging (bulk cartons or retail punnets, with or without your artwork), quantity, and your target delivery week within the season.</p>
